---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureSheetToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureSheetToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="excel-to-pdf">
  <PdfSecureSheetToPdf client:load source="excel" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>What this produces</h2>
      <p>
        The tool renders the values of your workbook as a clean, paginated table. It is not a screenshot
        of Excel and does not copy its styling. Column widths come from the cell contents, the first row
        can repeat as a header on each page, and long cells wrap and are not cut off.
      </p>
      <p>
        This suits the usual reasons to convert a spreadsheet: to send a table that the recipient can read
        but not edit, or to attach it where only PDFs are accepted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What it deliberately does not reproduce</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Feature</th>
              <th scope="col">Here</th>
              <th scope="col">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Cell fills, fonts, borders</td>
              <td>Not carried over</td>
              <td>
                An accurate style engine is a full spreadsheet application. Print to PDF from Excel if you
                need the exact look
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Charts and images</td>
              <td>Not included</td>
              <td>They are drawing objects, not cell data</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Formulas</td>
              <td>Cached results only</td>
              <td>Nothing is recalculated; you get what the file stores</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Merged cells</td>
              <td>Value in the top-left cell, blanks elsewhere</td>
              <td>The file stores the merge that way</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Print areas and page breaks</td>
              <td>Ignored</td>
              <td>Row height and paper size control pagination</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Hidden rows and columns</td>
              <td>Included</td>
              <td>Check for them before you share sensitive data</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Cached values, and why that is the honest answer</h2>
      <p>
        An .xlsx file stores two things for each formula cell: the formula and the value Excel last
        calculated for it. This tool prints the stored value. It does not evaluate <code>VLOOKUP</code>,
        <code>XLOOKUP</code>, <code>SUMIFS</code> or any other formula.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is almost always what you want, because it is what the workbook says today. The exception is a
        file written by a script or an export library that did not store the cached values: those cells are
        blank. If you see unexpected blanks, open the workbook in Excel or LibreOffice, save it once, and
        convert again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Making a wide table fit</h2>
      <p>Try these in order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Keep fit-to-width on. Column widths are measured from the content, then scaled proportionally so
          the whole table fits inside the margins. Nothing is cut off at the page edge.
        </li>
        <li>
          Switch to landscape. An A4 page goes from 523 to 770 usable points, 47 per cent more.
        </li>
        <li>
          Reduce the font size. The slider goes down to 5 pt. At 6 pt a table is still readable on paper
          and easy to read on screen.
        </li>
        <li>
          Use A3. It has about twice the area of A4, and most office printers can shrink it to A4 if
          necessary.
        </li>
        <li>
          Delete the columns you do not need to send. This is almost always the real fix.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        One very wide column cannot squeeze the others: each column is limited to 45 per cent of the usable
        width before scaling, so a free-text notes field wraps and leaves room for the columns next to it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Fonts, and the character limitation</h2>
      <p>
        All PDF readers include the same fourteen standard fonts, so a document that uses them needs no
        embedded font data and stays very small. These fonts use WinAnsi encoding, which covers Western
        European alphabets, including English, French, German, Spanish, Italian, Nordic and Portuguese.
      </p>
      <p>
        Greek, Cyrillic, Hebrew, Arabic, Devanagari, Chinese, Japanese and Korean have no glyphs in those
        fonts. To render them, the tool would have to embed a Unicode font fetched from a server, and this
        site never does that. Unsupported characters therefore become a question mark, and the result
        reports <strong>the exact number of replacements</strong>, so you know immediately and not after
        you send the file.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Multiple sheets</h2>
      <p>
        For workbooks with several sheets, a picker appears. Choose one, or all of them. With "all sheets",
        each table has its sheet name in bold above it and starts on the page where the previous one ended,
        with no forced page break, so small lookup tables do not each use a whole page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sheets with no rows are skipped, so there are no empty pages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Related conversions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/csv-to-pdf">CSV to PDF</a>: the same renderer, for a plain text file.</li>
        <li><a href="/excel-to-csv">Excel to CSV</a>: when a script will read the data.</li>
        <li><a href="/pdf-to-excel">PDF to Excel</a>: the reverse direction, by column clustering.</li>
        <li>
          <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a>: if the document must be smaller.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
